---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import { LlmQueryFlow } from "../../components/LlmQueryFlow";
import NumberedItem from "../../components/NumberedItem.astro";
import NumberedList from "../../components/NumberedList.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="How They Function - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="how-they-function">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        Every time you start a conversation with an AI tool, you're interacting
        with a fresh instance that knows nothing about you. It hasn't read your
        previous conversations. It doesn't know what project you're working on.
        It doesn't remember that brilliant prompt you crafted yesterday. Every
        single chat starts from absolute zero.
      </p>

      <p>
        Many tools like ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini save your conversation
        history and layer on extra features to give the AI a "memory" and the
        appearance of knowing you. However, this is not the same as the AI having
        a memory. It won't have any intuition about you, your projects, or
        strictly look into the memory every time.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the most important mental model shift when working with AI
        tools: <strong class="text-stone-800"
          >there is no persistent memory</strong
        >. The model is loaded, your message is sent to it along with any
        conversation history the application has been keeping, it generates a
        response, and then it effectively forgets everything. The next message
        you send? The application re-sends the entire conversation so far, and
        the model processes it all again from scratch.
      </p>

      <h3>
        The Conversation Loop
      </h3>

      <p>
        Here's what actually happens each time you send a message:
      </p>

      <NumberedList>
        <NumberedItem number={1}>
          <strong class="text-stone-800"
            >The application collects the full conversation</strong
          >
          — your system prompt (if any), every previous message, and your new message
          — and sends it all to the model as one big block of text.
        </NumberedItem>
        <NumberedItem number={2}>
          <strong class="text-stone-800"
            >The model processes everything at once</strong
          > and generates a response, one token at a time, streamed back to you
          as it's produced.
        </NumberedItem>
        <NumberedItem number={3}>
          <strong class="text-stone-800"
            >The response is added to the conversation history</strong
          > by the application. The model itself retains nothing.
        </NumberedItem>
        <NumberedItem number={4}>
          <strong class="text-stone-800">Next message?</strong> The whole process
          repeats. The full conversation (now longer) is sent again. The model reads
          it all again. It has no memory of having just generated the previous response.
        </NumberedItem>
      </NumberedList>

      <LlmQueryFlow client:visible />

      <p>
        This means the "continuity" you experience in a conversation is an
        illusion maintained by the application, not the model. The application
        is doing the remembering. The model just sees a block of text and
        generates what comes next.
      </p>

      <p>
        This goes deeper than the conversation history. Even the internal
        workspace the model builds up while reasoning through your message (the{" "}
        <a href="/fundamentals/what-is-an-llm" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">J-space</a>{" "}
        we touched on in the last chapter) exists only for that single response
        and is gone before the next one. There's no version of the model quietly
        carrying anything over between messages.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Why this matters">
        <p>
          If you close a chat and open a new one, you're starting completely
          fresh. The model won't know what you were working on, what decisions
          were made, or what approach was agreed upon. This is why providing
          good context at the start of each conversation is so important and
          why tools like Claude Code and Cursor invest heavily in features that
          automatically gather and re-send relevant context for you.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>
        What "Stateless" Means in Practice
      </h3>

      <p>
        Because the model is stateless, a few practical consequences follow:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Long conversations degrade
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            As a conversation grows, the model has to process more and more text
            each turn, and eventually it runs out of room. The raw model handles
            this bluntly: once you exceed its limit, the oldest text is simply
            truncated. Tooling has improved a lot here. Claude Code, Claude, and
            ChatGPT now compact long conversations by summarizing earlier turns
            rather than dropping them outright, so more of the original intent
            survives. It's still lossy, so if you've ever noticed an AI
            "forgetting" a detail you mentioned 20 messages ago, this is why. The
            next chapter digs into how compaction works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Starting fresh is often better
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            A clean conversation with well-crafted context will almost always
            outperform a long, meandering one. Don't be afraid to start new
            chats. Copy in the relevant context, state what you need clearly,
            and you'll generally get better results than pushing an old
            conversation further.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Instructions need to be restated
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            If you told the model to "always respond in bullet points" at the
            start of a long conversation, that instruction might get lost as the
            conversation grows. System prompts and tool configurations handle
            this by ensuring key instructions are always included, but in a
            plain chat, early instructions fade.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Tools vs. Models</h3>

      <p>
        It's worth distinguishing between the <strong class="text-stone-800"
          >model</strong
        > (the LLM itself) and the <strong class="text-stone-800">tool</strong> (the
        application you interact with). The model is stateless and generates text.
        The tool wraps the model with features: conversation history, file access,
        web search, code execution, memory systems, and more.
      </p>

      <p>
        When Cursor reads your codebase and sends relevant files to the model
        along with your question, that's the tool doing work the model can't do
        on its own. When Claude Code remembers your project structure across
        sessions via CLAUDE.md files, that's application-level memory, not model
        memory. Understanding this distinction helps you use these tools more
        effectively — you'll know what the model needs to be told versus what
        the tool handles for you.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="The practical takeaway">
        <p>
          Treat every conversation as if the AI has amnesia: because it does!
          Front-load your context, keep conversations focused, and don't
          hesitate to start fresh when things get muddled. The next chapter
          covers context windows in detail: how much the model can "see" at
          once, and what happens when you exceed that limit.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
